#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1671fb4f-937f-5a3c-85b7-7f54f35ea543" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Increasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73f4e014-8da4-5b62-bdfc-ec411a3b0d4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (\\frac{1}{e},e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402999} {:answer/id #uuid "76661337-48e8-57d3-8bea-4b19ca760485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403000} {:answer/id #uuid "09678020-9243-55b4-90ec-2108b1b5d475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (e,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403001} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c67b87-f457-538d-b30f-ec78659d8d53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,\\frac{1}{e})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403002} {:answer/id #uuid "94cfcda9-0e4d-516f-8a47-33ef687cc9c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (\\frac{1}{e},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94cfcda9-0e4d-516f-8a47-33ef687cc9c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (\\frac{1}{e},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403003} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e24e608-be13-57b9-a057-7f31c98ba594" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "680534fd-cbc6-5a80-9a00-31c6d6540a49" :question/math-academy-id "q-48860" :question/problem "![](images/61/61b89de34a6900d2ea39e5c2af141b3353e9ca790ac2748d0fc6d4d578127f93.png)\n\nFind the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ (plotted above) is strictly increasing." :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x)$ using the product  rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1>0.\n$$\n\nSolving $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=-1$ gives the critical point\n\n$$\nx={e}^{-1}\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{e}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that this critical point is a minimum. We also see that the slope of the tangent lines is negative to the left of the critical point and positive to the right of the critical point. \n\nSo, we summarize the sign of the derivative in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\begin{aligned}x & (0,\\frac{1}{e}) & \\frac{1}{e} & (\\frac{1}{e},\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ \\\\ {f}^{′} & - & 0 & +\\end{aligned}\\right|\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the interval $(\\frac{1}{e},\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b44a8701-760d-5416-b893-3d71c14b1c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403005} {:answer/id #uuid "f8053b86-fe4b-5739-9cec-901362aaacea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{5}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403006} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d608f5-8e00-5335-be54-24bd868e707f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2)$ and $(3,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403007} {:answer/id #uuid "98965492-25f8-5080-841b-0c0fba95d92f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(5,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403008} {:answer/id #uuid "55ec6a9f-7101-57ed-aa04-3627e626014b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8053b86-fe4b-5739-9cec-901362aaacea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{5}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403006} :answer-field/id #uuid "097a6ef4-4c48-59ce-a36f-fbe1f855b309" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e226491-3c89-5e59-9e2d-888677b66097" :question/math-academy-id "q-48909" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=(3t-5)^{2}$, where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((3t-5)^{2}) \\\\  & =6(3t-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ 6(3t-5) & <0 \\\\ 3t-5 & <0 \\\\ 3t & <5 \\\\ t & <\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (0,\\frac{5}{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Are Neither Local Minima nor Local Maxima"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf4c1754-7444-5b3e-9adb-b999feb5d3c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403012} {:answer/id #uuid "7f42e6d0-acca-52f9-b706-07727a97f26d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403013} {:answer/id #uuid "8d7adadf-13b8-55d4-850e-7d569a1d23dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403014} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6512f4-e752-579c-b598-1bc6f0bc5096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403015} {:answer/id #uuid "93c32a85-16bf-5a02-bc28-d95959b46df9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d7adadf-13b8-55d4-850e-7d569a1d23dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403014} :answer-field/id #uuid "2db08ffa-94f5-5d8e-9eee-e4055ee3e403" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66a1c6ee-aed3-5592-86e6-e5621e001888" :question/math-academy-id "q-49032" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2x+4}$ defined for $x\\in (-2,\\infty ),$ which of the following statements are true?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\cdot (2x+4)-2({x}^{3})}{(2x+4)^{2}}=\\frac{4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}}{(2x+4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(-2,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}}{(2x+4)^{2}}=0 \\\\ 4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}(x+3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x)$ on $(-2,\\infty ).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{4(-1)^{3}+12(-1)^{2}}{(2(-1)+4)^{2}}=2>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{4(1)^{3}+12(1)^{2}}{(2(1)+4)^{2}}=\\frac{4}{9}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30d03c60-3151-59b6-b7b9-fd72aa928ebd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403019} {:answer/id #uuid "5f900a01-8dad-54ab-bb5e-4a84ad557187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}{sin}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403020} {:answer/id #uuid "a782534b-97b6-5072-b2d6-441797eca761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403021} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc1f314-0209-5118-9c75-ea26340d8758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403022} {:answer/id #uuid "704fb728-1e9d-5aae-b2b0-2c48902cd828" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a782534b-97b6-5072-b2d6-441797eca761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403021} :answer-field/id #uuid "606ee288-277b-5ad8-a08e-806ce45db66b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb0ef12c-9b61-582b-acd5-7493f7def056" :question/math-academy-id "q-49054" :question/problem "![](images/47/47849c87c3c8cf9008b6531e740c6fc57058905cf17e31e4c93c479f337f0fbd.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y={e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ that is closest to the point $(1,0)$ satisfies the equation\n\n$$\n2x-2+=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(1,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{({e}^{x}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-2+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ 2x-2+{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4b4b7f1-de87-51e2-9ed4-2f2cacacaf29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is a piecewise function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403025} {:answer/id #uuid "84f08e7a-96c7-57e5-8318-9498325cc5c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403026} {:answer/id #uuid "de5ec02c-27a2-5d7d-8fe3-b0875d4201ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(-1)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403027} {:answer/id #uuid "60166612-0904-52f0-96ae-69b9e3a2f0dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403028} {:answer/id #uuid "3265516b-d79e-586e-a42e-0a19b7a4fb6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)\\ne f(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84f08e7a-96c7-57e5-8318-9498325cc5c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403026} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c9ecb96-b0c8-577b-ba89-04233ae88d06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca98ec7d-0763-558c-b634-f1da93c05079" :question/math-academy-id "q-51054" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-1?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+x, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+1, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(-1)=(-1)^{2}+1=2,$ so $f(-1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}({x}^{3}+x) \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+(-1) \\\\  & =-2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51c15d7a-cd9c-58df-a750-97df0b695c64" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Concavity From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ea1609e-b20a-54a8-915b-5dda82c99744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403031} {:answer/id #uuid "e543617b-6351-5c17-a3f6-e2802c494112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,1)$ and $(1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403032} {:answer/id #uuid "c568cebe-030f-5cf8-b856-afd17200378c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,5)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403033} {:answer/id #uuid "3d165dca-76a6-5440-b19b-8c808cb523b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,1)$ and $(3,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403034} {:answer/id #uuid "523960b9-215b-5ec5-9e2e-830b99e6b9c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,3)$ and $(1,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ea1609e-b20a-54a8-915b-5dda82c99744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403031} :answer-field/id #uuid "56e62435-0b4a-5c11-a074-69bafa619ea5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd3f3945-1d74-5b64-9e26-f19b51b6c5b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-51250" :question/problem "![](images/23/237c36808ded541ed264826866db3d2774dc2d5212f1292f21a6c25b3b07d0e1.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-3,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved downward like a frown ($⌢$) over the interval $(1,3).$\n\n![](images/a7/a7d9436275984c12271f2c737eb005e1eaab5b308c2204040e7aff5e87faffb3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51c15d7a-cd9c-58df-a750-97df0b695c64" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Concavity From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e973d9a-0f09-5900-bf22-eef66722e865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403038} {:answer/id #uuid "47cac611-9920-5c71-952f-dd8cb39ccdf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403039} {:answer/id #uuid "4042983e-d1df-528a-8fe4-25f17290d359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403040} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b0f23a-5c95-5614-8f48-1c8705ee4818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403041} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e429b3-2ae0-5777-a461-216e6c6244c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ and $(1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3e429b3-2ae0-5777-a461-216e6c6244c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ and $(1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403042} :answer-field/id #uuid "38c59fe2-2779-5ba6-9c0d-69d20ff0ea27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1beed24-efd2-587e-8b4f-b044374db684" :question/math-academy-id "q-51432" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c8969fae3e21c8e5f5019509636e2307211921d742e6f067d9ee1c50499541b.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-3,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved upwards like a smile ($⌣$) over the intervals $(-3,-1)$ and $(1,3).$\n\n![](images/ae/ae9ce6ca2597a4d5d1ca150c1e97d7c7a0a35f59fe5d6421b75203c6882d2052.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0cd1bb61-2770-5096-83d6-d1e4a7189e9a" :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing \"Or Equal To\" Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6c66b28-305f-5672-966e-0edbadb3f68b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge 3x+1 \\\\ y\\ge 2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403045} {:answer/id #uuid "22d57f63-0dab-54f1-a015-47cd4fa3ad27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge 3x+1 \\\\ y\\le 2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403046} {:answer/id #uuid "c282cd0f-a1b4-575a-bd8d-6fbc31f00808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge 3x+1 \\\\ y<2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403047} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc09857-90e2-5fa6-8e10-a5896664af24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le 3x+1 \\\\ y\\le 2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403048} {:answer/id #uuid "076f9837-2705-5d0a-9c57-71c316b16391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le 3x+1 \\\\ y\\ge 2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6c66b28-305f-5672-966e-0edbadb3f68b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge 3x+1 \\\\ y\\ge 2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403045} :answer-field/id #uuid "6391aa03-c66c-510d-8de3-714552c986fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9187138f-ae2f-5a56-952a-6135b67724bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-52413" :question/problem "![](images/75/75a7f3bfae55a97fbc0c05e2e0356d21de6ceabcc82ac3e1c965838a636f5ecc.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Consider the line $y=3x+1.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 3x+1.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=2.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge 3x+1 \\\\ y\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbd52408-5f2f-58c9-9a88-bd992fe8752c" :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Strict Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "599f73e4-3ca9-5922-b9a5-51c11d904876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y<2x+6 \\\\ y>5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403052} {:answer/id #uuid "88ca59a7-54b0-51b6-8e85-e1665efeaa4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y<2x+6 \\\\ y<5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403053} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2ec7bb-7f05-5512-a12e-01138a6573c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le 2x+6 \\\\ y\\le 5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403054} {:answer/id #uuid "17af44de-3d26-5293-8028-23cd473e2e0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y>2x+6 \\\\ y<5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403055} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ebc338-a51c-54a2-9008-89b303d45e94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y>2x+6 \\\\ y>5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88ca59a7-54b0-51b6-8e85-e1665efeaa4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y<2x+6 \\\\ y<5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403053} :answer-field/id #uuid "057029ec-da64-5909-af01-3dd51581c107" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d48a81ca-ca4e-5354-bd11-f73315ba7d7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-52461" :question/problem "![](images/ba/ba01cf5268f2bd62835c925a5eb793c0d98663948899c9e9aba2eeacd37ff30a.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Consider the line $y=2x+6.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<2x+6.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=5.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y<2x+6 \\\\ y<5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cfc862ea-961c-50e4-a89b-10c5ef88d851" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center and Radius of a Circle Given Its Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcb5d640-6be6-55e0-9c19-beb9cef25ff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius is $169$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403059} {:answer/id #uuid "50693ac5-91ac-507a-9bea-2faceed1fd57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(1,1)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{13}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403060} {:answer/id #uuid "184b7f5f-0cc8-5cea-85cb-9c3c0e38ca1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius is $26$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403061} {:answer/id #uuid "3b62671f-e9f6-5c77-a8c0-9cf013f52c90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(1,1)$ and the radius is $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403062} {:answer/id #uuid "152e2df1-5743-5c39-8f54-57e7e1870f14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius is $13$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "152e2df1-5743-5c39-8f54-57e7e1870f14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius is $13$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403063} :answer-field/id #uuid "aca4e605-47ce-5294-954a-2fe2d10aec36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13dc47a1-270d-56c2-8deb-b105337fbddb" :question/math-academy-id "q-54202" :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=169?$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the origin is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=169$ represents a circle that is centered at the origin, with radius\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =169 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\ r & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we disregarded the negative solution for the radius since the radius of a circle must be positive.\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(0,0)$ and its radius is $13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d0e48e3-39ca-551a-80a4-dc7ff7855197" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect with a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da491dad-4f1d-55ca-bfae-d14b2a451b28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ and $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403066} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a92487-d3ea-562f-9904-5f62363a5252" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403067} {:answer/id #uuid "521e3958-944c-5902-9f7b-658d7a27ed83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle and the line do not intersect" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403068} {:answer/id #uuid "5735b4a8-63cf-532a-a1bc-1c227aacb6c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403069} {:answer/id #uuid "5e53871a-4f10-5856-a7d3-60a65cf5aa04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "521e3958-944c-5902-9f7b-658d7a27ed83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle and the line do not intersect" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403068} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea675cb1-71da-58ac-9964-d0086c97dbd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6de12d86-c297-540f-8fcc-949f18bd056f" :question/math-academy-id "q-54242" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ intersects the line $y=-x+3.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=-x+3$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(-x+3)^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+{x}^{2}-6x+9 & =5 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x+5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =16-40 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-24<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $2{x}^{2}-4x+5=0.$\n\nHence, we conclude that the circle $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ does not intersect the line $y=-x+3$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](images/85/85c9bea054f2e246d62864b022e53a6f1d23886b0f0c9f8c80c8e0a5b1be9cd8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f4e3599-287a-56a3-8f1b-9e7033803e0a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Acceleration on a Speed-Time Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1719b7cb-bb0a-512e-b4d0-2db46619b09d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $70\\,\\text{s}$ to $80\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403073} {:answer/id #uuid "538466f6-26ea-5ceb-93d4-bf50e00e92bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $30\\,\\text{s}$ to $80\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403074} {:answer/id #uuid "0140ed8a-92fd-5826-aecd-2f790dfa3107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $30\\,\\text{s}$ to $70\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403075} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc348c6-96de-5702-89fd-0eab3b40e299" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $0\\,\\text{s}$ to $70\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403076} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6efc88-a3cc-5c91-8eab-852285448c53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $0\\,\\text{s}$ to $30\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b6efc88-a3cc-5c91-8eab-852285448c53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $0\\,\\text{s}$ to $30\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403077} :answer-field/id #uuid "f193901b-aa88-5598-b883-d5711c39904d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cca94ba1-21b2-5b66-81e8-07381d34c86b" :question/math-academy-id "q-59711" :question/problem "![](images/40/40143c20622029d70a978310a7d38bc41d7b8411151d3d5f61a3b280e5807841.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph shown above represents the motion of an object. At what times is the object accelerating?" :question/worked-solution "The object is accelerating when its acceleration is positive. In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line. So, we need to find the times for which the slope is positive.\n\nWe see from the graph that the slope is positive from $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ to $t=30\\,\\text{s}.$ Therefore, the object is accelerating in this time interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5408298f-7273-5f8e-b08d-eb9052a72e9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=2\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403080} {:answer/id #uuid "55c297c3-ce19-541b-8f15-0e1cff269acc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=\\frac{3}{4}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403081} {:answer/id #uuid "18ad6631-816d-504b-86a5-d5fe74a70d5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403082} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc7cd2a-4140-5747-8785-3c2be9dddaf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,x=3\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403083} {:answer/id #uuid "ef782508-1876-5861-8688-45bcea667f16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18ad6631-816d-504b-86a5-d5fe74a70d5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403082} :answer-field/id #uuid "838eec31-f001-5f3e-95ff-aee58026f6f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1ac9dcc-ee5b-5438-8680-0a3c7af8b759" :question/math-academy-id "q-60145" :question/problem "The equation $-20{x}^{2}+60x-41=0$ can be written in the form $20(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-20{x}^{2}+60x-41) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 20{x}^{2}-60x+41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{x}^{2}-60x+41 & =0 \\\\ 20{x}^{2}-60x & =-41 \\\\ 20({x}^{2}-3x) & =-41 \\\\ 20[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}] & =-41 \\\\ 20[{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}] & =-41\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-20\\cdot \\frac{9}{4} & =-41 \\\\ 20{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-45 & =-41 \\\\ 20{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =45-41 \\\\ 20{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=4.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{20} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{5}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{3}{2}| & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{5}} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae3561cc-7a36-53c3-8463-a08b60f2e422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403087} {:answer/id #uuid "9c0c8fbf-a096-54ef-a916-0cf279874a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403088} {:answer/id #uuid "84b429b9-e24f-5b71-bb06-7a7076b1bd11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403089} {:answer/id #uuid "c808c746-284d-54ac-ab48-93af25059dd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=\\frac{17}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403090} {:answer/id #uuid "06498b0f-3db5-5fe1-b4f1-27d65e29f54e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06498b0f-3db5-5fe1-b4f1-27d65e29f54e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403091} :answer-field/id #uuid "5281993a-3636-5f8e-a209-1d71b7850d4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a7884f70-6bc5-5884-9b9e-474178b4363b" :question/math-academy-id "q-60356" :question/problem "The equation $-3{x}^{2}-2x+6=0$ can be written in the form $3(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q-p.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-3{x}^{2}-2x+6) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2x-6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+2x-6 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2x & =6 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}x] & =6 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}x+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}] & =6 \\\\ 3[{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}] & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{9} & =6 \\\\ 3{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3} & =6 \\\\ 3{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & =6+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & =\\frac{19}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{1}{3}$, $q=\\frac{19}{3}.$ Finally,\n\n$$\nq-p=\\frac{19}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{18}{3}=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "257b907c-70c1-5bbd-a6d2-297d51325d1f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4729e5c5-d121-5071-90ac-905326cae80d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-1,\\,x=1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403093} {:answer/id #uuid "148ab9f1-4a75-549b-80f9-e24ca927be0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=11,\\,x=-1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403094} {:answer/id #uuid "a69056b8-e131-5a3d-920b-99466a245297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=11,\\,x=1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403095} {:answer/id #uuid "0a32de8d-323c-57e3-a4c6-10f2b23289bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=11,\\,x=1\\pm \\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403096} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a7de99-c75b-55c2-bfe2-679645b43eb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-1,\\,x=1\\pm \\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a69056b8-e131-5a3d-920b-99466a245297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=11,\\,x=1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403095} :answer-field/id #uuid "08090f79-b14c-5148-89c6-fad864772e14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "404aac0a-c4f3-5720-8a82-6f8114d49cae" :question/math-academy-id "q-60360" :question/problem "The equation $2{x}^{2}-4x-9=0$ can be written in the form $2(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-4x-9 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x & =9 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-2x) & =9 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{(1)}^{2}-{(1)}^{2}] & =9 \\\\ 2[{(x-1)}^{2}-1] & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-1)}^{2}-2\\cdot 1 & =9 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2}-2 & =9 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2} & =9+2 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2} & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $q=11.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-1)}^{2} & =11 \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2} & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-1)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f692eea3-1a29-5067-86dd-16a56be40703" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e4d353e-3cdf-51e1-9b07-e23aab389446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\,020$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403100} {:answer/id #uuid "2473a95c-bc78-544c-ba5f-dd05a6bde4c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\,140$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403101} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b5c16a-3e3a-56c0-92e2-62eb6e146444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$350$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403102} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9877a7-08c8-5095-8bfe-0888fe2d868a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$250$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403103} {:answer/id #uuid "c032b177-d808-54a3-9954-343c6079d4c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\,160$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c032b177-d808-54a3-9954-343c6079d4c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\,160$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403104} :answer-field/id #uuid "31fad44a-2df1-5489-abcd-c6f3bbfc46a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d586afc6-dd55-5a3f-a72a-36aae91b1948" :question/math-academy-id "q-62825" :question/problem "A tank has an inlet and an outlet. When the inlet is turned on, the rate at which water flows into the tank governed by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=9{t}^{2}+18,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in liters per minute and $t>0$ is the time in minutes.\n\nWhen the outlet is switched on, water leaves the tank through it at a rate of $14$ liters per minute.\n\nThe inlet is switched on, and the water level increases to $120$ liters, at which point the outlet is also switched on. How much water is in the tank $10$ minutes after switching on the outlet, given that the outlet is switched on at $t=0?$" :question/worked-solution "Water enters the tank at a rate of $9{t}^{2}+18$ liters per minute and leaves at a rate of $14$ liters per minute. Therefore, the **rate of change of volume** (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-14 \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}+18-14 \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total volume** of water in the tank after $t$ minutes (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =120+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(9{x}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the volume of water after $10$ minutes, we substitute $t=10$ into the above and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(10) & =120+{\\int }_{0}^{10}(9{x}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =120+{[3{x}^{3}+4x]|}_{0}^{10} \\\\  & =120+[(3(10)^{3}+4(10))-(0)] \\\\  & =120+3040 \\\\  & =3\\,160\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7eacaa-2cf2-5cae-a40c-737c98124bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial Using the Fast Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17920adf-88bb-56b9-a038-f51d4eaf2572" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403107} {:answer/id #uuid "52631d39-c84b-531c-bafd-9cf41fb2419f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403108} {:answer/id #uuid "2740bd1c-bbc0-58ec-b556-0c638035ad73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403109} {:answer/id #uuid "207bf098-fd82-59bd-a19d-bfd61799988a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403110} {:answer/id #uuid "26e1821b-7fbe-5fd7-9873-8c9812b489bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52631d39-c84b-531c-bafd-9cf41fb2419f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403108} :answer-field/id #uuid "9976b442-22bd-51db-888b-a94056eb3810" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1f59ac7-fe59-5414-84e5-39e2cb13a6a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-64198" :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\nI. $f(y)=5{y}^{2}$\nII. $g(x,y)=2{x}^{3}y+x{y}^{4}$\nIII. $h(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}-xyz$" :question/worked-solution "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(y)$ is homogeneous of degree $2.$ It is a polynomial of degree $2,$ with no linear or constant term.\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $5.$ However, it also contains a term ($2{x}^{3}y$) that is of degree $4.$ Therefore, not all of its terms are of degree $5.$\n\n- The function $h(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$ It is a polynomial of degree $3,$ and all of its terms are of degree $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e4b1bd9-a788-5338-b6a3-0321efca35f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-{x}^{2}+3x-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403113} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3a8c12-b522-5cd4-80bc-d4e5e857d33f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}={x}^{2}+x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403114} {:answer/id #uuid "56190e71-ba59-515b-a03d-ccc2b7e5bdcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2{x}^{2}-x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403115} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1d2035-a1c4-5242-b59f-70997c2bf914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}={x}^{2}-3x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403116} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb4f8b5-aafd-5f68-a0c9-e62da167d11f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-{x}^{2}+2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfb4f8b5-aafd-5f68-a0c9-e62da167d11f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-{x}^{2}+2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403117} :answer-field/id #uuid "62409f98-64c5-5617-a551-69fb536c4027" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43efe1d0-c638-55a1-8bd9-f43f26e2bc24" :question/math-academy-id "q-64206" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12y=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-6x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +4(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-12(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n-12\\alpha {x}^{2}+(8\\alpha -12\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +4\\beta -12\\gamma )=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-12\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 8\\alpha -12\\beta =-32\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +4\\beta -12\\gamma =-30\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =2,\\gamma =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ed6eeb5-a646-5f47-b6d8-f1078f550376" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Factors Giving an Exact ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89d8404e-4aaf-511f-9859-6d27274954ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403119} {:answer/id #uuid "9862b857-6ea9-52a3-a713-1d7ee260467b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403120} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf5884c-aaa5-5d80-8e2b-0a81f4471125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403121} {:answer/id #uuid "5c20f220-acdf-509c-abcb-d33e114a770c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403122} {:answer/id #uuid "796ba2c3-bb1b-5078-8683-f57cc7daf2a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "796ba2c3-bb1b-5078-8683-f57cc7daf2a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403123} :answer-field/id #uuid "96536a92-d62c-5223-bb4d-c2d51d3aea5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c92b66b-ccec-5e6d-8f75-9e3b9cdb1f7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-64207" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8$\nII. $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2$\nIII. $2x+7$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=4{y}^{3}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{y}^{3})=0\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=2x+7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+7)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2x+7$ gives an exact equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01e7705-e8a6-5712-ad9a-2fc77ee3cdec" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing and Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f573070e-3c1e-51db-89e2-b8464c92accf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=4x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403125} {:answer/id #uuid "5ab75f36-dd12-5220-bb2c-6a0d2db3072b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403126} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e81942-358d-5aaf-a5f2-5e1f2ec31b30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2{x}^{2}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403127} {:answer/id #uuid "05bdb4b7-bec0-5820-a812-ddc518d8373b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403128} {:answer/id #uuid "977556d9-51ca-596c-9fa9-5b596de1cf76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=5{x}^{2}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "977556d9-51ca-596c-9fa9-5b596de1cf76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=5{x}^{2}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403129} :answer-field/id #uuid "db7e9798-e426-59e9-86d1-aae4a4100379" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c49ef0d7-77b7-5469-92f2-ccc1a74380b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-64236" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=10{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x},$ we have that $\\lambda =1$ is the double root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10{e}^{x}.$  In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $1$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha ({x}^{2}+4x+2){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha ({x}^{2}+4x+2){e}^{x}-2(\\alpha ({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{x})+(\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{x}) & =10{e}^{x} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+4x+2-2{x}^{2}-4x+{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{x} & =10{e}^{x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{x} & =10{e}^{x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =10 \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{x}^{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d86b46c0-e30d-525b-b84e-f8296c78aa8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Affine Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5720f24-4834-5f92-98ef-62d15aa34022" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=\\frac{x}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403132} {:answer/id #uuid "94298549-9f11-59e3-9778-348dfb75c6dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2x-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403133} {:answer/id #uuid "a894006f-0a61-540c-bca8-073ac57ffb84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-x-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403134} {:answer/id #uuid "98f1d505-2a8f-586d-b7f5-1495b711f3f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403135} {:answer/id #uuid "9f49a9b7-19a3-54bd-a9db-ec8551633352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98f1d505-2a8f-586d-b7f5-1495b711f3f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403135} :answer-field/id #uuid "090c78d3-3a0c-5fa4-b1fc-517e968e51e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d511e4f-8803-5021-b5c2-53a7b4de5acf" :question/math-academy-id "q-64285" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y=-5x+6\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$  and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-4\\alpha -5(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-5x+6\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n-5\\alpha x+(-4\\alpha -5\\beta )=-5x+6\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha =-5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -4\\alpha -5\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a81eb74c-2bdd-58d3-9e9a-8979bdb86dd3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse Laplace Transform of a Function Using the Scalar Multiplication Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0cbb343-c5b6-5165-b8dd-e5752f164d76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403139} {:answer/id #uuid "a59bbe62-a208-5df3-8661-8ea4f1d189bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403140} {:answer/id #uuid "0832e7dd-c627-5e7c-947a-b791faee1d4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403141} {:answer/id #uuid "92948375-0cdf-554d-8f1f-7d2651438c62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403142} {:answer/id #uuid "f1fbd783-28f7-58f0-9642-0a8534ea1dee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a59bbe62-a208-5df3-8661-8ea4f1d189bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403140} :answer-field/id #uuid "caf67a00-882f-5531-9d3d-d727009d9381" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48e33a66-e4ca-5916-8f3e-f14a3e1757b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-64351" :question/problem "Find the inverse Laplace transform for $F(s)=\\frac{4s}{{s}^{2}+9}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{3}^{2}}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by applying the inverse Laplace transform to the above result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the multiplicative property of the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot {L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}}=4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{4s}{{s}^{2}+9}}=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb4b6a5f-9aed-5dad-9356-4038bf9c0acf" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse Laplace Transform Using Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e19fd0b-b240-5849-a448-96c0e258519e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}={e}^{2t}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403145} {:answer/id #uuid "a65647aa-7466-568f-bd00-1d8ba00ad061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}={e}^{-t}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403146} {:answer/id #uuid "be86f51b-975e-5a8f-ab4e-d91c7209b21a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}={e}^{-2t}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403147} {:answer/id #uuid "96be269f-2b9f-59fc-b9c7-1595a7320a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}={e}^{6t}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403148} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed1044f-f4c3-5331-8dd9-20f0c0018f5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}=6{e}^{t}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96be269f-2b9f-59fc-b9c7-1595a7320a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{L}^{-1}{F(s)}={e}^{6t}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403148} :answer-field/id #uuid "67e77dc5-1be6-5ae7-84fd-9a901ee251ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d006ce4c-8de4-5385-bde9-8b9759c49cd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-64381" :question/problem "Find the inverse Laplace transform for $F(s)=\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-6s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write $F(s)$ as a sum of partial fractions, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-6s}=\\frac{6}{s(s-6)}=\\frac{1}{s-6}-\\frac{1}{s}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{6t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-6},\\,s>6 \\\\ L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the addition property of the Laplace transform, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-6}-\\frac{1}{s}} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-6}}-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s)=\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-6s}$ is $f(t)={e}^{6t}-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "963c27b5-72b1-58a7-97c4-b639bb22723c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants I and II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403152} {:answer/id #uuid "40cb6aef-e725-5253-b719-75e17c581e3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403153} {:answer/id #uuid "2224451a-8e17-51d0-986f-1214ed5940f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403154} {:answer/id #uuid "0e4a2436-2852-516f-92f8-f717d63fcdbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403155} {:answer/id #uuid "90c1d347-78c0-572b-be64-becc6182a361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2224451a-8e17-51d0-986f-1214ed5940f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403154} :answer-field/id #uuid "99087b5c-e287-5db8-8067-9d5a74a34eb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81353a59-cc3b-54d7-bb2e-9d6179d10f11" :question/math-academy-id "q-64676" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =-{135}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{135}^{∘}$ by *adding* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- $-{135}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={225}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${225}^{∘}$ is coterminal with $-{135}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/11/1196aea617827f2883e059747f037997741570eac382b309391a88978a861246.png)\n\nWe see that ${225}^{∘}$ lies in quadrant III. Therefore, $-{135}^{∘}$ also lies in this quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e72d2ef-11e7-5363-bac5-671eb90f3369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403159} {:answer/id #uuid "ccaa6bd8-19ca-50f7-afe3-f13abc30ccd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403160} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6b6c4f-6ff5-5186-9c43-254f26013671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403161} {:answer/id #uuid "b616503f-5c11-5640-98b1-3558d18557b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403162} {:answer/id #uuid "f41f9c75-55f9-59f2-9b27-7b3d50783eb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e72d2ef-11e7-5363-bac5-671eb90f3369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403159} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d8f144d-6bde-515d-8710-0773c908d13d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cc83dd4-931c-5537-b469-11f079199b4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-64677" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =-{210}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{210}^{∘}$ by *adding* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- $-{210}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={150}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${150}^{∘}$ is coterminal with $-{210}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/78/788edaa48306f8370f9780cdeda956a85c34b76bd6ec5a028d9d38d425cbc5b9.png)\n\nWe see that ${150}^{∘}$ lies in quadrant II. Therefore, $-{210}^{∘}$ also lies in this quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcb5277-6d80-5cea-9688-ddb98f434093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa7b08f4-fd50-5010-9da5-db54d62d9cfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ or $-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403166} {:answer/id #uuid "4baca1b8-62e7-5f52-af30-1fc3884ec93d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ or $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403167} {:answer/id #uuid "989a9e5a-82d4-559e-b64e-15fdf78c6beb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ or $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403168} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbdf5da-2897-576f-842c-af25e23e73b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ or $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403169} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a7a515-e8e3-5996-959a-f3908110ac5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-6$ or $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4baca1b8-62e7-5f52-af30-1fc3884ec93d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ or $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403167} :answer-field/id #uuid "9aa513b8-ca41-5963-9d1c-ec92073b4073" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a341ef36-052b-58a5-8e5a-622fd4d5ea6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-64701" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-15y=0$ where $y=y(t).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}-15{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -15) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -15 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -5)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4fd239d-c7ac-543b-9af2-dff66d73ae14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403173} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a82ac0-c0b1-522e-87c4-1a14814f3bc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403174} {:answer/id #uuid "77412101-bb3d-58bf-a714-56bc3f99924a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403175} {:answer/id #uuid "686aa6c6-ecea-5827-a8b9-ee253c9c74ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403176} {:answer/id #uuid "20044cf4-5820-566d-9d4e-d2e750da3ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77412101-bb3d-58bf-a714-56bc3f99924a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403175} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea86f69a-d990-5653-ae40-c11a11e9a47b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0b4e05c-493c-513f-8d82-76dddaa02634" :question/math-academy-id "q-64953" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}={e}^{x}({u}^{′}+u) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={e}^{x}({u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}+u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}+u)-2{e}^{x}({u}^{′}+u)+{e}^{x}(u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}+u-2{u}^{′}-2u+u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}{u}^{″} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=0\\,⟹\\,w={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}({c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\cdot x=x{e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a34d342c-e7fc-581b-b3d1-ed6a49f1a62f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb5fc021-9def-5f26-bdda-dd8981cd9477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403180} {:answer/id #uuid "d4805135-9684-5a00-9f95-f2b36b056956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403181} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc9a92e-cdeb-574d-afe7-3878b1c63788" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403182} {:answer/id #uuid "9a12800f-c92a-5174-aec3-8b80600d5d2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403183} {:answer/id #uuid "27287a40-3f38-5322-bb12-ef884d3f2246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a12800f-c92a-5174-aec3-8b80600d5d2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403183} :answer-field/id #uuid "bee37669-e7fb-516e-95fe-1e5da2a5227f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b765011e-9d19-55a7-93ef-fd380a1d1bd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-65012" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+4x{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,y(0)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3$\nII. $4{y}^{″}+y=0$\nIII. $x{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-y=0,\\,y(0)=-4,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-4$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not a boundary value problem. The differential equation is combined with two conditions, but both conditions are given at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$). This is an **initial value problem.**\n\n- The second system is not a boundary value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=1$)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7619454-7a2b-5c0b-927c-24dca2e564c3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions of Partial Derivatives at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7ceb90c-5f78-5aac-95c4-9b929295f4eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403187} {:answer/id #uuid "86e71b49-0357-56df-ae34-bbfae281c19a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403188} {:answer/id #uuid "df88aa45-c9fd-5b07-9a7f-2504e2e6c593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403189} {:answer/id #uuid "778ed61b-59cd-569d-ac0c-5ae90967b20f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403190} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7e7d1b-3293-5604-9415-c90b70a5c91a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df88aa45-c9fd-5b07-9a7f-2504e2e6c593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403189} :answer-field/id #uuid "29a5142e-9f89-5edb-9583-bae503ae820a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b61d9c66-e5e5-507e-a184-52505bf6709b" :question/math-academy-id "q-65130" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ evaluate ${f}_{yxx}(1,1)$ if ${f}_{xy}(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}(2+xy).$" :question/worked-solution "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxx}(x,y) & ={f}_{xyx}(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{xy}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{xy}(2+xy)) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}(2+xy)+{y}^{2}{e}^{xy} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}(3+xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{yxx}(1,1)=(1)^{2}{e}^{1\\cdot 1}(3+(1)(1))=4e\\,\\,\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403192 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43ee40fd-e452-5593-9a35-7c46d1f0cca0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Constants in First-Order Partial Derivative Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1daec83e-8dc8-5ec1-b22a-c0bb43879285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2$, $b=-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403194} {:answer/id #uuid "cb09c9e5-8229-57b4-8d2f-394d5ee4343c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2$, $b=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403195} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf3cb46-39db-594a-a510-ab78bee329e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2$, $b=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403196} {:answer/id #uuid "602715ca-8049-5747-9cc7-65702c17fe69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=4$, $b=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403197} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3f48df-ee89-5d2f-a388-8701be207f24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1$, $b=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1daec83e-8dc8-5ec1-b22a-c0bb43879285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2$, $b=-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403194} :answer-field/id #uuid "36a84ad8-7e07-5e98-8973-bf508e59e4e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee4afd99-ea98-5301-bd22-e1e65a00ab8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-65134" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(x,y)=a{y}^{2}x-3y,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y+bx.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(a{y}^{2}x-3y) \\\\  & =2axy-3 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}y+bx) \\\\  & =4xy+b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2axy-3 & =4xy+b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $xy$ and constant terms, we see that $a=2$ and $b=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111362 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67692569-e3a4-51e8-99ac-79d8b3081363" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ddd0a51-517b-5bff-8885-5e9848dd116c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403200} {:answer/id #uuid "b63c130c-1a35-56cf-9996-10fcf4c15056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403201} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf8afbd-b37b-5a43-bb4a-92e18c922a16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403202} {:answer/id #uuid "451708fb-03c6-54ef-9db8-451358a59324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403203} {:answer/id #uuid "027fa94d-be6c-535b-aca3-7f2637554bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "027fa94d-be6c-535b-aca3-7f2637554bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403204} :answer-field/id #uuid "1896dae5-9abf-5c8e-9b50-3e787f54d53c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "183017f0-e71f-5632-89e1-4603485e8f26" :question/math-academy-id "q-65744" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\nI. $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2})$\nII. $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\nIII. $(0,2\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(0,2\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(2\\pi ,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=2\\pi $ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,2\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64edd709-3f6c-59e1-ae1f-bca25eca0483" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reflection of a Sine Curve Over the Horizontal Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa65de1-f8c2-59ee-a58c-73e8d5a35da2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-sin⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403206} {:answer/id #uuid "cae2feac-73a9-5382-be98-e2d0b9a31076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pi sin⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403207} {:answer/id #uuid "d5459411-109c-5634-a6d6-08426f206d22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-sin⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403208} {:answer/id #uuid "7886e8be-97bf-51de-9431-947f4fe26d47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=sin⁡(x)-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403209} {:answer/id #uuid "d253f678-7f7d-5e04-b080-f88bfe3b102d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=sin⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fa65de1-f8c2-59ee-a58c-73e8d5a35da2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-sin⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403206} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5a960d3-9ccd-5921-846a-d374278a4922" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d5ac861-a6f5-5aad-8010-c1a0229f1d0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6580" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2fec23f57e68ac65bf05662e70fba9ac5872f77d63a55f89f7e90b9f0b9bfa24.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the graph shown above?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/02/0204cfb247273efc20a31366dcb84096d630aa6dbe90c94a8802834205bbdd56.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-sin⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-sin⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af0b18ca-54a2-5a24-a670-faa311096abc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25e238b4-329e-5c12-b4b1-e63b207eb270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403212} {:answer/id #uuid "8025c2ee-4e82-5585-96b1-f42102f0be89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403213} {:answer/id #uuid "6be7901d-4564-527c-9c84-01fa86346cc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403214} {:answer/id #uuid "4267965c-34c0-543d-ba09-0d28cebb16a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403215} {:answer/id #uuid "31e4cd2c-a53f-53dc-915b-aad9bad5a954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6be7901d-4564-527c-9c84-01fa86346cc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403214} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7bbe6a0-52dc-5f94-aa4f-a035168ac90e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c5ae160-d78c-57fc-8666-ed9ed6778702" :question/math-academy-id "q-65828" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n\nI. $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2})$\nII. $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$\nIII. $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{7\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range.   $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af0b18ca-54a2-5a24-a670-faa311096abc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6bfad6c-2814-5c54-aba4-2b500b7ae26b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403218} {:answer/id #uuid "206105e2-44d4-5574-89ca-ddf2279a77dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403219} {:answer/id #uuid "d1df0086-7f48-50cb-8ceb-18066bc865de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403220} {:answer/id #uuid "29558d43-c76b-586a-9425-52db91027e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403221} {:answer/id #uuid "c6000078-e4e1-5811-8829-82307d46879b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6bfad6c-2814-5c54-aba4-2b500b7ae26b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403218} :answer-field/id #uuid "389f0e82-b83e-5348-b35b-7ad0561fa28a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7680c22b-fa61-58ad-8d65-6ff8999855b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-65947" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n\nI. $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\nII. $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\nIII. $(1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.  \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(1,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Graph of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0415b469-b139-5e1a-a412-34227a3897af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403224} {:answer/id #uuid "23feee12-2b3b-5956-a66b-2f5a51f37208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403225} {:answer/id #uuid "d3754b87-9f75-54d9-9a94-d542f1aaa946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403226} {:answer/id #uuid "4215ac44-f44e-59a5-8b63-fb6307287e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403227} {:answer/id #uuid "46e2afd4-b53a-541a-99a5-3d5382aea9b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0415b469-b139-5e1a-a412-34227a3897af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403224} :answer-field/id #uuid "28f631db-f510-51e8-a456-9f86ddcaf626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b81fb291-176e-5b1d-97d6-f57c92461362" :question/math-academy-id "q-65990" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\nI. $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\nII. $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\nIII. $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80449399-3ae4-593f-8c3b-00bcdcc99727" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Outside of the Range of Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36f640cb-1d0e-55cf-82bf-2bc2df4f2cbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403231} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4fdfad-f0bb-539a-b3dd-5135ecf4149a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403232} {:answer/id #uuid "de09ab34-6244-5de9-adc0-7d2808c13ffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403233} {:answer/id #uuid "a347e69f-18e6-5a4a-896e-70ebdae9cbe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403234} {:answer/id #uuid "87a5d244-0e82-5176-9600-2dd50bb45515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de09ab34-6244-5de9-adc0-7d2808c13ffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403233} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ca79eb9-6a7d-56ad-a7b2-6517a75087f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28fa7313-ebe9-5114-aa43-a48667cbf78a" :question/math-academy-id "q-66005" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\nI. $4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\nII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+10=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\nIII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-6$" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{3}$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=10$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=6$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation I has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c8c1d8-2662-5c15-b084-084f9e930141" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b7d8b31-ae04-5df6-aeb1-961f777e33b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-108,\\,x=10\\pm 8\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403238} {:answer/id #uuid "3477ce1f-9124-5c8e-aee7-0c19aaa37d5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-108,\\,x=-8\\pm 10\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403239} {:answer/id #uuid "516e48e9-f368-58d1-b1c7-a413f32276d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-92,\\,x=-8\\pm 10\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403240} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9c6c12-d688-5f40-9928-9159b2b770a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-92,\\,x=10\\pm 8\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403241} {:answer/id #uuid "a06bc635-fc47-5897-92f1-2184d35dc008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-92,\\,x=-8\\pm 8\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "516e48e9-f368-58d1-b1c7-a413f32276d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-92,\\,x=-8\\pm 10\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403240} :answer-field/id #uuid "042ad804-2249-5399-b919-45947b7e0d31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f70143e0-3f9c-51d2-946d-0c2579968816" :question/math-academy-id "q-66435" :question/problem "The equation ${x}^{2}+16x+164=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "We first move the constant to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+16x+164 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+16x & =-164\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+16x & =-164 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 8x+{8}^{2}-{8}^{2} & =-164 \\\\ (x+8)^{2}-64 & =-164 \\\\ (x+8)^{2} & =-100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=8,\\,q=-100,$ and $p+q=-92.$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+8)^{2} & =-100 \\\\ x+8 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-100} \\\\ x+8 & =\\pm 10\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =-8\\pm 10\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=-8+10\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=-8-10\\,\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "046ec56a-2154-55dd-81a9-19e9359e8a35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403245} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe7f7f0-cfc0-5858-9600-b3b2c717f329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403246} {:answer/id #uuid "813d4ece-3add-5aa3-95e4-73b6c64ea24a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403247} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbfd34e-4c5f-5bda-b5d4-833bc6a12f00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403248} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc0a540-ce92-5907-a74f-b7d9a91e069f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "046ec56a-2154-55dd-81a9-19e9359e8a35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403245} :answer-field/id #uuid "85ad16e3-732f-5dec-b28a-7752a9983d6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b9771ce-36d1-5cb6-a7ee-84e40d892ce6" :question/math-academy-id "q-66508" :question/problem "![](images/23/239490fd3be930412d4f67ff89244607a5019d91f201bc4e579410b5610a18ed.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](images/c7/c728d173c13a1e7b3bcad0e9f0284c97f2ca075c795dcb1f38ed086c19cc0504.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that: \n\n- For graphs I and IV, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graphs I  and IV show one-to-one functions. This means that  these functions are invertible.\n\n- For graphs II and  III, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a8c6b41-dda4-5b72-b40c-3e85cba087db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403252} {:answer/id #uuid "8c44c0b8-a324-51aa-8160-2529aa798669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403253} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ff6185-1935-54af-a0dd-0d7567723234" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403254} {:answer/id #uuid "22145362-fe93-590d-8788-5b69cf80e6c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403255} {:answer/id #uuid "5297b063-4a7c-5a1f-aec3-8f8e6212d515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5297b063-4a7c-5a1f-aec3-8f8e6212d515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403256} :answer-field/id #uuid "6127ad12-ca4b-5bf9-9f70-8f5bdf2f9c6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d080acd-916c-5650-8a33-fa75e4ad9891" :question/math-academy-id "q-66509" :question/problem "![](images/be/be5b7625ad6f540f6769b121f52ccad69b669dbf764fc3bb68f89c99f066f145.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](images/bf/bfc7d94de67ceeb0c4aaa0988c117fce7f15242c2f3d223eecd8f0eb77135d7e.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that: \n\n- For graph IV, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph IV shows a one-to-one function. This means that  the function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, II and  III, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-